Modular Architecture Setup
A modular architecture divides an application into separate modules based on responsibility. Each module handles a specific part of the application, which improves maintainability, testing, scalability, and code reuse.

Project Structure
fastapi_project/
├── app/
│   ├── main.py
│   ├── core/
│   │   └── config.py
│   ├── models/
│   │   └── user.py
│   ├── schemas/
│   │   └── user.py
│   ├── repositories/
│   │   └── user_repository.py
│   ├── services/
│   │   └── user_service.py
│   └── routes/
│       └── user_routes.py
│
└── tests/
    └── test_users.py

1. Application Entry Point
# app/main.py

from fastapi import FastAPI
from app.routes.user_routes import router as user_router

app = FastAPI(
    title="Modular FastAPI Application",
    version="1.0.0"
)

app.include_router(user_router)


@app.get("/")
def root():
    return {"message": "API is running"}

2. Model
The model represents the application's data.

# app/models/user.py

from dataclasses import dataclass


@dataclass
class User:
    id: int
    name: str
    email: str

3. Schema
Schemas define the structure of API input and output.

# app/schemas/user.py

from pydantic import BaseModel, EmailStr


class UserCreate(BaseModel):
    name: str
    email: EmailStr


class UserResponse(BaseModel):
    id: int
    name: str
    email: EmailStr

4. Repository
The repository handles data access.

# app/repositories/user_repository.py

from app.models.user import User


users: list[User] = []


def create_user(user: User) -> User:
    users.append(user)
    return user


def get_user(user_id: int) -> User | None:
    return next(
        (user for user in users if user.id == user_id),
        None
    )

5. Service
The service contains business logic.

# app/services/user_service.py

from app.models.user import User
from app.repositories.user_repository import create_user, get_user
from app.schemas.user import UserCreate


def create_new_user(data: UserCreate) -> User:
    user = User(
        id=1,
        name=data.name,
        email=data.email
    )

    return create_user(user)


def find_user(user_id: int) -> User | None:
    return get_user(user_id)

6. Routes
Routes handle HTTP requests and responses.

# app/routes/user_routes.py

from fastapi import APIRouter, HTTPException

from app.schemas.user import UserCreate, UserResponse
from app.services.user_service import create_new_user, find_user

router = APIRouter(
    prefix="/users",
    tags=["Users"]
)


@router.post("/", response_model=UserResponse)
def create_user(data: UserCreate):
    return create_new_user(data)


@router.get("/{user_id}", response_model=UserResponse)
def get_user(user_id: int):
    user = find_user(user_id)

    if user is None:
        raise HTTPException(
            status_code=404,
            detail="User not found"
        )

    return user

7. Route Tests
# tests/test_users.py

from fastapi.testclient import TestClient

from app.main import app


client = TestClient(app)


def test_create_user():
    response = client.post(
        "/users/",
        json={
            "name": "John",
            "email": "john@example.com"
        }
    )

    assert response.status_code == 200
    assert response.json()["name"] == "John"


def test_get_user():
    response = client.get("/users/1")

    assert response.status_code == 200
    assert response.json()["email"] == "john@example.com"

Architecture Flow
Client
  │
  ▼
Routes
  │
  ▼
Services
  │
  ▼
Repositories
  │
  ▼
Database

Benefits
Routes handle HTTP requests.

Services handle business logic.

Repositories handle data access.

Schemas validate API data.

Models represent application data.

Tests verify individual modules.

This separation keeps the FastAPI application modular, reusable, testable, and easier to maintain.
